## Original Content
<p>As federal borrowing rules change, graduate education has a chance to move beyond sticker price and focus on flexibility, transparency, and long term value. For students, the question is not only how to pay for a degree, but whether the degree is designed to be worth the investment.</p>
<p>The post <a href="https://blog.coursera.org/as-u-s-graduate-financing-changes-affordability-by-design-matters-more/">As U.S. graduate financing changes, affordability by design matters more</a> appeared first on <a href="https://blog.coursera.org">Coursera Blog</a>.</p>

## Summary
As U.S. federal borrowing rules for graduate education change, there is a growing emphasis on flexibility, transparency, and long-term value over traditional sticker prices. The article argues that affordability by design is key, urging students to consider whether degrees are designed to be worth the investment.

## Key Points
- Federal borrowing rules for graduate education are evolving, impacting financing options.
- There is an opportunity to shift focus from costs to flexibility and transparency.
- Affordability by design is becoming more important in evaluating graduate programs.
- Students should assess the long-term value and design of degrees, not just how to pay for them.


